      "title": "Assistant Director of Academic Success Center",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\n\nPosition Title: Assistant Director of Academic Success Center Department: Academic Success Center Reports To: Director of Academic Success Center Appointment: Grant funded, full-time, 40 hours per week (12-month position), On Campus Status: Exempt The Assistant Director of the Academic Success Center is a grant-funded position responsible for ensuring quality of academic success services with the objective of advancing Bradley University's goals of improving success and retention.  The Assistant Director will collaborate with relevant stakeholders’ university-wide to develop and deliver academic success services tailored toward students' learning needs, including, but not limited to, individual academic coaching, development and delivery of study skills materials, and student outreach. This position also provides support for students in the Academic Recovery Program (ARP), as well as academic coaching for students in any of the university's five colleges.  The Assistant Director will also work collaboratively within the Academic Success Center and throughout the university to assist with case management for students identified as at-risk, onboarding students during summer new student orientations, and developing and implementing guidelines for the ethical use of artificial intelligence in academic support programming (e.g., academic coaching, peer tutoring, Supplemental Instruction, and Peer-Led Team Learning). The Assistant Director reports to the Director of the Academic Success Center within the Division of Academic Affairs.  Essential Duties and Responsibilities Academic Skills Programming Development  Provide individualized academic coaching to help students identify strengths and barriers.  Develop, implement, and expand individual academic coaching and study skills workshops.  Identify evidence-based practices in academic coaching and study skills support and provide training and support in student-facing settings.  Develop and deploy study skills resources, such as time management and test preparation planners, for use throughout the Academic Success Center and in appropriate university settings.  Serve as a connecting point between individual students and various campus professionals and offices. Support Student Success  Support the Academic Success Center in creation of progress reports by using third-party platforms (e.g., Navigate360) to generate student lists during the first and third quarter of the semester.  Analyze data from progress reports to identify early warning indicators.  Use analytics dashboards in third-party platforms (e.g., Navigate360) to assess population health and historic course outcomes.  Assist university personnel in supporting at-risk students to improve success and retention.  Collaboration with Academic Success Center (ASC) initiatives Participate in staff meetings and committees assigned. Recommend guidelines for ethical use of artificial intelligence in academic support programming. Assist with case management and support for students in the Academic Recovery Program (ARP). Assist with office events, including recruitment activities, and other activities as required. Support new student orientation initiatives within the Division of Academic Affairs. Other duties as assigned. Requirements  Bachelor’s degree in education or related field from an accredited institution  Exceptional interpersonal, organizational, and communication (verbal and written) skills  Detail-oriented, possesses excellent problem solving and analytical skills, and willingness to embrace change management  Capable of working on multiple projects simultaneously  Ability to build positive relationships within the Bradley University community  Preferred Requirements  Experience working with academic support initiatives including study skills development and peer-led academic support  This is a full-time, grant-funded position. Continuation of this role is strictly contingent upon the availability and renewal of grant funds. In the event that grant funding is reduced, terminated, or ceases, the position may be eliminated or modified accordingly. Some evening and weekend work is required. Qualified candidates should submit a cover letter, resume and complete contact information for a minimum of three professional references with telephone numbers.  Please submit all materials at the time of initial application .  Employment with Bradley University is contingent upon successful completion of a criminal background check. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Bradley University offers a competitive benefits package including health, dental, vision, life and disability coverages, FSA, HSA, and retirement plan options. Bradley also offers significant higher education cost savings for employees and dependents through the Tuition Remission program. Information regarding our benefits can be found here:  https://www.bradley.edu/offices/business/human-resources/benefits/ Bradley University is a top-ranked, private university in Peoria, Illinois, offering nearly 5,000 undergraduate and graduate students’ opportunities, choices, and resources to build their futures. Innovation, action, and collaboration drive Bradley students to generate ideas and create solutions that remake the world around them. Our comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate academic programs includes business, communications, education, engineering, fine arts, health sciences, liberal arts and sciences, and technology. Bradley University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer. The administration, faculty and staff are committed to attracting qualified candidates from underrepresented groups.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=df6f93d4-2277-4999-ac63-88a55668ffd3&ccId=9200110706773_2&jobId=9201859468820_35&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistive Technology Coordinator, Student Access Services",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\n\nPosition Title: Assistant Director for Student Access Services/Assistive Technology Coordinator Department: Student Access Services Reports To: Director of Student Access Services Appointment: Grant funded, full-time, 40 hours per week (12-month position), On Campus Status: Exempt Bradley University invites candidates for the 12-month, grant funded position of Assistive Technology Coordinator within the   Office of Student Access Services (SAS). The Assistive Technology Coordinator supports the University's commitment to accessibility and inclusion by coordinating assistive technology resources and services for students with disabilities .   The Assistive Technology Coordinator works collaboratively with students, faculty ,  staff, and campus partners to identify and implement technology solutions that address individual accessibility needs and  promote equitable access to academic programs and university resources. This position provides individualized technology consultations, training, and technical support while assisting with the implementation of  a assistive technology accommodations, including alternative format materials and accessible testing software. The Assistive Technology Coordinator reports to the Director of Student Access Services within the  Division of Academic Affairs.   The successful candidate will demonstrate strong technical, organizational, interpersonal, and problem-solving skills, along with a commitment to student-centered service, confidentiality, and inclusive practices.    Essential Duties and Responsibilities  Coordinate assistive technology solutions to meet students' individual accessibility needs.  Provide individualized consultations, training, and technical support to students on assistive technology software, hardware, and accessibility resources.  Coordinate alternative format accommodations, including electronic textbooks (e-text), accessible course materials, and other digital resources. Assist students with approved note-taking software.  Coordinate and support technology-related testing accommodations, including text-to- speech, speech-to-text, screen readers, and other accessible software. Collaborate with the SAS Testing Coordinator, faculty, and campus partners to ensure appropriate technology is available and functional.  Troubleshoot assistive technology issues, coordinate software installation and updates, and maintain assistive technology equipment and resources within Student Access Services.  Collaborate with students, faculty, Information Technology, and other campus partners to identify and address technology-related accessibility barriers.  Develop and deliver training, workshops, and presentations for students, faculty, and staff on assistive technology, alternative formats, and accessibility best practices.  Create and maintain instructional materials and resources to promote awareness and effective use of assistive technology.  Maintain documentation related to assistive technology services, alternative format accommodations, equipment, and resource utilization while ensuring student confidentiality.  Assist with equipment inventory, purchasing recommendations, and resource management.  Evaluate the effectiveness of assistive technology resources and services and contribute to the development and improvement of departmental policies and procedures.  Stay informed about emerging assistive technologies, accessibility standards, and best practices in higher education.  Participate in departmental initiatives, professional development, and other duties as assigned.  Required Qualifications  Bachelor's degree in a related field or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience.  Experience working with individuals with disabilities, preferably in higher education or a related educational setting.  Knowledge of assistive technology, accessibility tools, and resources that support individuals with disabilities.  Strong written and verbal communication skills, organizational abilities, and problem- solving skills.  Ability to work independently and collaboratively with students, faculty, staff, and campus partners.  Ability to maintain confidentiality and exercise sound professional judgment.  Demonstrated commitment to inclusive practices and working effectively with individuals from diverse backgrounds.  Preferred Qualifications  Two or more years of experience in disability services, assistive technology, or accessibility support, preferably in higher education.  Experience evaluating, implementing, and troubleshooting assistive technology software and hardware.  Familiarity with electronic textbooks, note-taking software, text-to-speech, specch-to- text, and other accessible testing technologies.  Knowledge of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), Section 504 of the  Rehabilitation Act, and applicable accessibility standards.  Experience developing and delivering training or professional development opportunities for faculty, staff, or students.  In order to meet the needs of the university, the university retains the right to assign duties and requirements to employees, determine and change starting times, quitting times and shifts and change appointment specifications.  Mental/Physical Requirements  The selected candidate must have the mental and physical capabilities to perform the essential functions of the position with or without reasonable accommodation. Work is performed in a normal office environment with little exposure to outdoor temperatures or dirt and dust. Working conditions are typically quiet.  This is a full-time, grant-funded position. Continuation of this role is strictly contingent upon the availability and renewal of grant funds. In the event that grant funding is reduced, terminated, or ceases, the position may be eliminated or modified accordingly.  Some evening and weekend work required. Qualified candidates should submit a cover letter, resume and complete contact information for a minimum of three professional references with telephone numbers.  Please submit all materials at the time of initial application .  Employment with Bradley University is contingent upon successful completion of a criminal background check. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Bradley University offers a competitive benefits package including health, dental, vision, life and disability coverages, FSA, HSA, and retirement plan options. Bradley also offers significant higher education cost savings for employees and dependents through the Tuition Remission program. Information regarding our benefits can be found here:  https://www.bradley.edu/offices/business/human-resources/benefits/ Bradley University is a top-ranked, private university in Peoria, Illinois, offering nearly 5,000 undergraduate and graduate students’ opportunities, choices, and resources to build their futures. Innovation, action, and collaboration drive Bradley students to generate ideas and create solutions that remake the world around them. Our comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate academic programs includes business, communications, education, engineering, fine arts, health sciences, liberal arts and sciences, and technology. Bradley University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer. The administration, faculty and staff are committed to attracting qualified candidates from underrepresented groups.",
